{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 as the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability in the Crusader states. He inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Due to his youth a regency was established led by his mother and later by the King of Jerusalem. Raymond spent much of his youth in captivity after being captured by Nur ad-Din Zengi in 1164. He was held for nearly ten years and his absence caused internal strife in Tripoli. His captivity weakened the county and allowed rival factions to gain influence.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nUpon his release in 1174 Raymond returned to Tripoli determined to restore order and strengthen his position. He quickly became a key political figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was appointed regent of the kingdom in 1174 after the death of Baldwin III and the minority of Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was one of the most powerful nobles in the Levant and often clashed with other nobles especially Guy of Lusignan who was married to Baldwin IVs sister Sibylla. Raymond opposed Guy’s rise to power and believed he was unfit to rule. He allied with the Templars and other barons to resist Guy’s authority. His leadership was critical during the early years of Baldwin IVs reign as he helped maintain stability in the face of Muslim threats.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond played a major role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was accused of negotiating secretly with Saladin which damaged his reputation among his peers. He was blamed for the defeat at Hattin and the subsequent fall of Jerusalem. After the battle Raymond fled to Tripoli and died shortly after in 1187. His death marked the end of a long and turbulent chapter in the history of the County of Tripoli. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV. Raymond III is remembered as a capable but controversial ruler who struggled to balance loyalty to the kingdom with the interests of his own county. His life reflected the complex politics of the Crusader states where alliances shifted rapidly and survival depended on diplomacy and military strength. His legacy remains debated among historians who view him as both a defender of Christendom and a pragmatic politician willing to make uneasy compromises.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -5.1973900554003194e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0013335392577573657}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.2305197237292305e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.01815054751932621}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -1.0251946150674485e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.011605959385633469}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.006394876167178154}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.08049094676971436}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -7.986990567587782e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009010545909404755}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00015805903240107}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.2543604902457446e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.3132783770561218}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0037307203747332096}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.7881233361549675e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.907346813